<h2>Agents That Write to the Ledger — Digits Write MCP and the Locked-Period Answer</h2>

<h3>Summary in three lines</h3>
<ul>
    <li>On October 13 the accounting SaaS company Digits launched <strong>Write MCP</strong>. Six months after opening its MCP server as &ldquo;read-only,&rdquo; it now lets customers' AI agents edit transactions, bills, invoices and the chart of accounts in the general ledger. It is free for customers.</li>
    <li>There are two safeguards. An agent acts only within the signed-in user's permissions, and any edit that touches a <strong>locked period</strong> goes to a review queue where an admin must approve it before it posts. The point of the launch is that the control sits <strong>in the ledger</strong>, not in a warning dialog on the client.</li>
    <li>QuickBooks, Xero, NetSuite and Dynamics 365 have also opened writes through MCP. The industry question has moved from &ldquo;should agents write?&rdquo; to <strong>&ldquo;where does the control live?&rdquo;</strong> Writes in open periods, agent identity, segregation of duties and prompt injection hidden in invoices still have no published answer.</li>
</ul>

<p>MCP (Model Context Protocol) is the standard protocol AI agents use to call outside systems as tools (recent changes to the spec are covered in <a href="#!news-20260814-auto-mode-stateless-mcp">Defaults That Stop Asking</a>). Until now, MCP servers for accounting and ERP have mostly pointed to &ldquo;read-only&rdquo; as their safety argument: an agent can look into the books and answer questions, but it cannot change a single line. Digits made the same promise when it launched its MCP server on April 21, saying &ldquo;all access is read-only by design, preserving ledger integrity.&rdquo; Half a year later the company went past that promise itself. This article covers what was opened, what holds it back, and where other accounting and ERP vendors place the same control.</p>

<h3>Who Digits is</h3>
<p>Digits is an accounting software company founded in 2018. In March 2025 it launched an &ldquo;Autonomous General Ledger&rdquo; and said openly that it wanted to replace QuickBooks in small-business accounting. The general ledger is the book where every transaction a company makes is collected by account; it is the core of any accounting software. At launch, small-business pricing started at $100 a month, and a co-founder of Xero joined the leadership team at the same time. The company has said it has raised close to $100 million.</p>
<p>Its pitch is that a machine does the bookkeeping. It says more than 90% of transactions are categorized and booked automatically, though the figure varies between sources (93% and 95%) and nobody has verified it independently. The product grew one step at a time: agents that run accounting workflows in June 2025, a read-only MCP server in April 2026, automated accrual schedules in May, and Write MCP in October. Automation that used to run inside Digits' own screens now opens the door to outside agents that customers bring with them.</p>

<h3>What agents can write, and what stops them</h3>
<p>Write MCP is a free update to the existing MCP server and works with any MCP-compatible tool such as Claude, ChatGPT or Cursor. It covers four areas.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What an agent can do</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Transactions</td><td>Recategorize, edit fields, fix split lines, <strong>write journal entries</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bills</td><td>Create, code, correct</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Invoices</td><td>Draft, update, clean up</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Chart of accounts</td><td>Add, rename, update accounts</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>A journal entry is the record that splits a transaction into debits and credits in the books. An agent that can write journal entries can create the numbers in the ledger directly. Whether agents can write to the vendor master is not mentioned anywhere in the announcement.</p>
<p>The safeguards come in two layers. First, the agent acts with the signed-in user's permissions, so it cannot reach data that person cannot reach. Second is <strong>Locked Periods</strong>, which shipped alongside. Closing is the procedure that finalizes a month or quarter so the books stop changing. Once a period is locked, no one, person or agent, can change it silently: every edit that touches it goes to a review queue, and an admin approves or rejects it before anything posts. According to Digits' help documentation the lock cascades, so locking a month also locks every month before it. Periods that are still open work as usual, with edits applied immediately.</p>
<p>Digits frames the difference from other accounting software this way. Most of them protect closed periods with a warning popup or a shared password; a popup won't stop an agent, and once an agent has the password the protection is gone. QuickBooks does have a closing-date password that blocks edits to earlier periods. It was built to catch human mistakes, so anyone who knows the password can open it. The company's CEO said: &ldquo;Giving an agent write access to the ledger is easy. Doing it safely is the hard part, and that's what accountants care about most.&rdquo;</p>

<h3>Where the industry puts the control</h3>
<p>Digits is not the only one opening writes. Major accounting and ERP vendors have released MCP servers, and many of them support writing. The difference lies in where they place the control.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Vendor</th><th>MCP offering</th><th>Writes</th><th>Where the control sits</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Digits</td><td>Hosted server, free</td><td>Transactions, bills, invoices, chart of accounts</td><td><strong>The ledger</strong> (approval queue for locked periods) + user permissions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>QuickBooks (Intuit)</td><td>Official open source, runs on the user's machine</td><td><strong>Read-write by default</strong></td><td>Environment flags turn off write, update and delete tools; the operator designs any approval step</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Xero</td><td>Official open-source server + hosted connection for Claude and ChatGPT</td><td>The open-source server goes as far as invoices, manual journals and payments; the hosted connection is read-only</td><td>Bills are created as drafts</td></tr>
        <tr><td>NetSuite (Oracle)</td><td>AI Connector Service</td><td>Create and update records</td><td>Role permissions; <strong>refuses to connect with administrator or full-permission roles</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dynamics 365 (Microsoft)</td><td>ERP MCP server, generally available</td><td>Create, read, update and delete through data entities</td><td>Role-based security; Copilot Cowork summarizes its planned writes and gets approval first</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SAP</td><td>Joule supports MCP and A2A</td><td>Confirmed only in partner implementations</td><td>Finance writes through the official S/4HANA MCP could not be confirmed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sage Intacct</td><td>AI Gateway and MCP server in 2026 R2</td><td>Could not be confirmed</td><td>Advice to start with a dedicated low-privilege user</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Douzone Bizon ONE AI PE</td><td>Standard MCP-based integration (Seoul city's administrative AI agent project)</td><td>Executes inside internal systems</td><td>Permission checks and execution history; no public endpoint for outside AI could be confirmed</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Regrouped by where the control sits, the table falls into three groups. The QuickBooks open-source server leaves control to the <strong>client</strong>: whoever runs the agent decides whether to enable writes and whether to ask a person before a change. NetSuite and Dynamics 365 put it in <strong>roles and permissions</strong>, applying the permission model ERPs have long used to agents as well, and NetSuite simply refuses connections from its most powerful roles. Digits puts it in <strong>the ledger</strong>: whoever arrives with whatever permissions, a closed period is locked at the data layer.</p>
<p>The third approach points the same way as a trend this wiki has noted elsewhere: judgment moves out of the agent and into places the agent cannot touch. It is the accounting version of the observation in <a href="#!news-20260820-who-makes-the-call">Who Gets to Make the Call</a> from August, that judgment doesn't disappear, it changes seats. A warning popup is a device for asking a person, so it has no force in front of an agent. A rule the server enforces doesn't ask; it blocks.</p>

<h3>What still has no answer</h3>
<p>The only published material is the company's press release and its reprints, so no one has independently checked the safety claims yet. Within that limit, there are five questions an ERP practitioner would ask.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Open periods.</strong> The approval queue applies only to locked periods. Most of a month's bookkeeping happens before close, so most of what an agent actually writes is applied without review. The mechanism is strong at protecting past periods but does not control the current month.</li>
    <li><strong>Agent identity.</strong> If an agent acts with a user's permissions, the change history may show the person's name. Whether agent actions are marked separately has not been disclosed.</li>
    <li><strong>Segregation of duties.</strong> Segregation of duties is the internal-control principle that separates the person who creates a transaction from the person who approves it, so no single person can rewrite the books. It is unclear whether an agent using one user's permissions can both create and approve. Amount thresholds, where entries above a set value need approval, are not mentioned either.</li>
    <li><strong>Reversal and evidence.</strong> How an agent's change is undone (deleted, or offset with a reversing entry) and what trail is left for auditors are not in the published material.</li>
    <li><strong>Instructions inside invoices.</strong> Bills carry text written by vendors. Prompt injection is an attack that hides instructions inside data a model reads in order to change what the model does, and scenarios in which instructions hidden in an invoice's signature block steer an accounting agent come up again and again in the security industry. The security company Forcepoint reported that among ten indirect injection payloads it found on the live web in April 2026, one was built to execute a $5,000 PayPal payment.</li>
</ul>
<p>Audit standards have not caught up either. According to practitioner write-ups, as of mid-2026 the PCAOB, which sets audit standards for US public companies, had no AI-specific standard, and auditors apply existing standards by analogy. The write-ups agree on one principle: a sentence like &ldquo;the AI cannot post a journal entry above a set amount without approval&rdquo; is not a control if it lives only in a policy document. It becomes a control when the system actually blocks it. Posting means committing a record to the ledger.</p>

<h3>What ERP makers should take from this</h3>
<p>Read from the seat of an ERP vendor, the launch leaves three points.</p>
<p>First, &ldquo;it's safe because it's read-only&rdquo; won't last long. Digits opened writes within six months, and the QuickBooks open-source server has had writes on by default from the start. What customers eventually want is not an agent that looks at the books but one that enters the vouchers for them.</p>
<p>Second, ERPs already have usable controls: splitting a voucher into a saved draft and an approve-and-post step, period-end closing, and approval routing. What Digits added was making the period lock hold even in front of an agent. Expose those same mechanisms through MCP as server-side rules the agent cannot get around, and the split can be finer: the agent goes only as far as creating draft vouchers, and a person approves. The five questions above work directly as a design checklist.</p>
<ul>
    <li>Does the agent have its own identity, recorded separately in the change history?</li>
    <li>Is the same identity prevented from both creating and approving?</li>
    <li>Does the server enforce amount-based approval?</li>
    <li>Are changes undone with reversing entries instead of deletion?</li>
    <li>Is text that comes from outside, such as the memo line on a tax invoice, treated only as data and never as instructions?</li>
</ul>
<p>Third, pricing is diverging. Digits gave Write MCP away for free and turned the agents customers bring into a sales channel. Microsoft charges 0.1 Copilot Credits each time an agent built outside Copilot Studio calls the ERP MCP server. Vendors now have to decide whether outside agents writing to their ERP are a cost or a source of revenue.</p>
<p>The <a href="#!dzp-overview">GitLab-based AI development knowledge platform proposal</a> on this wiki also starts from a read-only design in which AI only reads the source system. Read-only is a good place to start. When the day comes to move to writes, the design question will be which rules to build into the server rather than leave to the agent.</p>

